WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:10.940
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; At this moment, I very much
would like Howard and Carol to join us at

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:19.330
the table, and to introduce to the Board Sir
Howard Bernstein; not just Chief Executive

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:25.970
of Manchester City Council but the deity,
if I may borrow the phrase – certainly,

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:32.119
the inspirational leader – of what we are
being asked to approve finally today and which

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:38.149
will go into force tomorrow, which is the
quite innovative and exciting arrangements

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:44.350
for a new model of governance for Greater
Manchester in which responsibilities for health

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:49.280
and social care are brought together by a
combination of providers and commissioners

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.340
across Greater Manchester.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.039
Howard, first of all, can I thank you on behalf
of the Board for your leadership and for all

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:57.739
the work that has gone into getting us to
where we are.

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:01.460
To share in that, also Paul, who has been
leading for us.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:08.540
Secondly, I invite you to address us this
morning on progress and the journey so far.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:14.020
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; Thanks very much, Malcolm,
and thank you, everyone, for inviting me.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:19.659
What you have in front of you is, I think,
a very fair and balanced analysis of the journey

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:23.409
we have had since February of last year.

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:33.090
It has been a 13-month period of great interest
and, if I may say so, a very exhausting process

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.090
as well.

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:39.500
I would particularly like to thank both Paul
and Simon, because part of the memorandum

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:48.200
of understanding which we signed last February
created a central role for a devolution programme

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:54.450
board, which both Simon and I chaired, with
extensive support from Paul.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:02:00.930
That has enabled us to retain not only a very
clear focus about the priorities and all the

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:06.469
bread-and-butter stuff which we are going
to be talking about shortly, but more particularly

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:12.310
a very clear focus about how we create an
inclusive platform amongst our communities

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:19.690
and stakeholders around the essence of the
health and social care plan which we produced

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.390
last December.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:27.550
If you were looking at a broad context within
which this debate should be placed, I would

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:32.410
invite you to look at Greater Manchester’s
approach to collaboration over many, many

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:33.410
years.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:38.980
It has been a form of collaboration which
has worked across local authority boundaries

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:45.310
in Greater Manchester, recognising that Greater
Manchester is a functional economic geography:

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:52.530
ten individual local authorities working across
boundaries, and also, very specifically, working

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:59.770
across public services as well; recognising
that if we are going to create a city region

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:08.610
which is world class and competitive economically,
it also has to be financially sustainable

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.440
as well.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:16.470
In those terms, health, social care and other
public services and their interaction together

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:21.640
are a fundamental part of our growth and productivity
agenda.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:31.489
So when we in Greater Manchester signed the
now-famous devolution deal in October/November

00:03:31.489 --> 00:03:38.640
2014, it was not just about the traditional
areas of public-sector functions which were

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.150
covered in that agreement, such as housing
and planning and transport; all the traditional

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.129
areas you would associate with growth.

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:53.790
We also placed early years, health and work,
and health/social care integration at the

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.480
heart of that devolution agreement.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:04.090
Indeed, on the initiative of Malcolm and Simon,
with wider Government support as well, we

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:07.890
signed our MOU in February of last year.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:15.340
Inevitably, the last 12 months have seen,
I think, a very steady sequence of steps which

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:20.190
we have taken in order to secure the platform
for change which we now have.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:25.190
A lot of time was spent initially around governance,
establishing governance arrangements that

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:35.240
will not just encourage a partnership approach
amongst the 37 NHS stakeholders in Greater

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:43.590
Manchester but also actually create, within
those components of governance, platforms

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:49.660
for the CCGs to work together and platforms
for the providers to work together.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:56.370
I must pay respect to the leadership of some
of our CCG colleagues, particularly our providers

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:01.800
– Ann from Stockport, Hamish from Salford
– who have provided real leadership and

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:08.430
support within their own communities and with
their own networks, which has made it possible,

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:13.970
under the leadership of Lord Peter Smith,
the ‘lead leader’ for Greater Manchester

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:20.430
on health/social care, to create a very powerful
platform for collaboration and joint work.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:27.090
The analysis which you have before you shows
some of the key priorities that we have been

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:28.150
focusing on.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:33.190
Simon gave us advice early on that we should
not just be focusing around the production

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:39.570
of a long-term plan; we also needed, in order
to provide confidence, to have a series of

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.949
early implementation priorities.

00:05:41.949 --> 00:05:45.100
I think we delivered on that commitment.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:50.940
Look at paragraph 11 with some of the most
significant early priorities, which all fall

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.440
within the established priorities of NHS England.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:01.550
I would actually identify one which I had
been working on for a very long time – how

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:07.630
we place health at the heart of our academic
health and science system – which has been

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:13.039
a key priority for Manchester for many, many
years, building on the supreme talents and

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:19.020
assets we have through our universities, teaching
hospitals and innovation system.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:25.220
On the back of the work that we have done,
we have been able to provide a unified focus

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.890
for academic health science, now called Health
Innovation Manchester.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:34.560
We look forward not only to supporting the
development of new pathways and healthcare

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:40.150
models, but more particularly to addressing
the opportunities for driving the growth agenda

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.080
of our economy.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:46.030
Not just because of devolution but because
of the excellence of some of those assets

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:52.260
I mentioned, we are now one of the key focuses
for global healthcare companies wanting to

00:06:52.260 --> 00:07:00.470
understand what is going on in Manchester
and how they can participate in the process.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:08.199
Our particular approach has been around how
we understand what works at GM level and what

00:07:08.199 --> 00:07:11.539
can only work at locality level.

00:07:11.539 --> 00:07:18.199
All ten local authorities have been invited
to produce what we described as locality plans.

00:07:18.199 --> 00:07:25.610
Those locality plans will provide the essential
platform for securing the effective involvement

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:31.520
of communities, the third sector, local authorities,
commissioning providers and CCGs, and each

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:37.940
of those localities will have one single commissioning
model, which will not only address the importance

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:42.699
of integrated health/social care – we are
particularly interested in some of the conversations

00:07:42.699 --> 00:07:49.419
which Jane in particular was facilitating
earlier – but will extend to the wider public-sector

00:07:49.419 --> 00:07:50.629
reform programme.

00:07:50.629 --> 00:07:56.259
We believe that is absolutely essential in
order to underpin the sustainability and the

00:07:56.259 --> 00:08:00.599
fiscal outcomes which we are determined to
deliver.

00:08:00.599 --> 00:08:08.160
We also recognise that things have to be delivered
at scale – at GM level – as well.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:13.680
That is where a big effort is going to be
made around how we upgrade population health

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:21.300
and how we address the all-important requirements
of standardising acute and specialised care.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:27.000
If you look at your papers, you will see four
very bold transformation programmes, all of

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:30.150
which command the support of all of our stakeholders.

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:35.560
A radical upgrade in population health, which
if I may say so, is providing very interesting

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:41.770
debates throughout Greater Manchester at the
present time about how devolution of the type

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:47.610
we are discussing here today is also actually
creating the opportunity for local authorities

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:54.550
themselves to rethink their relationships
with their own communities, and what the communities

00:08:54.550 --> 00:09:00.920
need to do in order to improve their own life
chances and their own participation to effective

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:01.920
communities.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:08.580
We are looking at transformation in community-based
care support, where we are already starting

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:14.410
to deliver new primary care platforms operating
at scale, looking at what we mean by ‘neighbourhood’

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:20.150
– a working of no more than 50,000 people
– and what we mean by ‘integrated care

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:25.530
organisations’ – we call them ‘locality
platforms’; there are going to be ten, all

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:33.620
with very clear criteria – and how we actually
develop new opportunities for standardising

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:34.839
frameworks.

00:09:34.839 --> 00:09:39.130
Also, standardising acute and specialist care,
as I have said.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:43.750
We have got a great start with the implementation
of our Healthier Together programme, which

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:48.550
was even tested in the courts but thankfully
came through.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:52.510
One of the most pleasing aspects for me was
the way in which the NHS community and the

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:58.500
local authority community stood fast behind
the outcomes of that programme, demonstrating

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:06.130
the absolute value of evidence and the ability
to support programme outcomes with hard evidence;

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:10.790
and, of course, grasping the Carter review
in all its glory.

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:17.620
Indeed, we have a meeting with Lord Carter
sometime next week in order to review the

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:20.300
progress we are making.

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:22.470
Across the board, we are talking about enablers.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:26.370
I noticed you were talking about enablers
in your own business plan.

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:31.920
We got our business plan together last autumn,
looking at the clear work streams which are

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:39.160
absolutely essential, such as ICT and digitalisation,
in order to drive the standards we are doing.

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:42.950
The other point I should make is that, right
across the board with the Department of Health,

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:50.230
NHS Improvement and estates, we enjoy very
productive working relationships because we

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:56.779
recognise that how NHS Improvement supports
our process is going to be very important

00:10:56.779 --> 00:11:03.019
for the delivery of our overall programmes,
as of course is how we actually evaluate and

00:11:03.019 --> 00:11:08.370
support the reconfiguration of the NHS estate.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:14.220
What we also want to do where estates is concerned
is to not just look at the NHS estate in splendid

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.610
isolation.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:21.680
We want to address the interdependencies,
because there should be several interdependencies

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:27.760
between the NHS estate and the wider public-sector
estate which exists in Greater Manchester.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:32.240
So, a lot of progress is being made.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:37.089
We are also addressing what has been a particular
priority of mine.

00:11:37.089 --> 00:11:44.940
One of the lights of the spending review last
year, of course, was the result of Greater

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:52.720
Manchester, with Simon and Paul’s support,
securing access to a £450 million transformation

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:53.720
fund.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:59.950
We asked for £500 million but we have to
settle for £450 million.

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:05.550
Fundamentally, what we have done is to spend
a long time working through what the essence

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:13.250
of that transformation fund will be, and what
it needs to grasp in terms of the system outcomes

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:19.269
that are required at locality and GM level
if we are going to deliver the outcomes which

00:12:19.269 --> 00:12:20.269
we said.

00:12:20.269 --> 00:12:25.490
I believe it is very important, because whilst
we are retaining, quite rightly, the existing

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:32.250
accountabilities in relation to how funding
flows are used, the transformation fund starts

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:39.779
to give us the opportunity to re-regulate
the relationships between the individual stakeholders

00:12:39.779 --> 00:12:45.790
in Greater Manchester and the strategic partnership
board at Greater Manchester for the effective

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:48.320
execution of those transformation outcomes.

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:55.720
So we are looking at £450 million only in
the context of our transformation programmes.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:13:00.940
I think one of our big challenges, not just
over the coming months but for the rest of

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:07.000
this programme, is to ensure that the initiatives
that, at times, can be rolled out at national

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:12.400
level, are actually properly and fully integrated
with the transformation programmes which we

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:15.550
are seeking to deliver in Greater Manchester.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:23.720
I will let Paul talk through the detail, if
people wish, about the accountability arrangements

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:25.350
which we have noted.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:30.540
They are, I think, very succinctly summarised
in paragraph 30 of the paper.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:35.070
I think it is fair to say that they represent
a fair and accurate reflection of the key

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:41.420
principles which we agreed we would honour
in February of last year when we developed

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:46.850
the memorandum of understanding.

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:51.170
I would personally like to give thanks not
only to many colleagues who have supported

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:59.040
us in this task, but also to the ten local
authorities, the 12 CCGs and the 15 providers,

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:04.520
who have been steadfast in their commitment
to working collaboratively and inclusively

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:07.810
to get us to the point where we are today.

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:14.579
As we all recognise, the really hard work
probably starts tomorrow as we turn strategy

00:14:14.579 --> 00:14:17.130
into action and implementation.

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:18.130
Thank you very much indeed.

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:22.070
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Howard, thank you very much
for that overview.

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:26.490
Let me invite some questions and comments
from colleagues around the boardroom table.

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:31.829
Victor Adebowale&gt;&gt; I have been remiss; I should
have declared another interest at the beginning.

00:14:31.829 --> 00:14:42.029
I apologise, Chair, but I am the chair of
an organisation called Collaborate, oddly

00:14:42.029 --> 00:14:45.390
enough, which is working with the Manchester
devo system.

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:47.992
If I may, can I just make a point?

00:14:47.992 --> 00:14:48.992
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Yes, of course.

00:14:48.992 --> 00:14:52.850
Victor Adebowale&gt;&gt; It’s not really Q&amp;A – I
do not want to turn it into a Star Chamber

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:59.459
– but I was just curious about your point
in relation to what happens next.

00:14:59.459 --> 00:15:06.980
I am just wondering what you are learning
about the leadership behaviours?

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:14.779
Of course, for this to work, the leaders of
institutions will need to become responsible

00:15:14.779 --> 00:15:19.380
not just for institutions, but also for the
place, and I am just curious about what you

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:26.050
are learning in terms of the leadership behaviour
shifts; things that are helping those behaviours

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:29.699
move from where they are to where they need
to be, people dealing with ambiguity, and

00:15:29.699 --> 00:15:30.699
all that kind of stuff?

00:15:30.699 --> 00:15:34.910
Because, at the end of the day, it is going
to be the behaviours of leaders.

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:39.370
The other question I have, which you did refer
to – not a question, but a thought that

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:49.449
I have – is about the engagement of the
wider non-health sector.

00:15:49.449 --> 00:15:55.470
Not so much about whether you are engaging
with them, but what innovations you are expecting

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:56.470
from them?

00:15:56.470 --> 00:16:02.259
What could they do to be making this work,
given that a lot of the people at the sharp

00:16:02.259 --> 00:16:06.829
end of the health and social care challenge
in Manchester are actually dealt with and

00:16:06.829 --> 00:16:11.670
worked with by people outside the health and
social care sector formally?

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:14.360
I am sorry to go on.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:21.790
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; I think it would be fair
to say that changing behaviours and culture

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:25.829
is not something we just snap our fingers
over.

00:16:25.829 --> 00:16:34.839
Encouraging leaders of organisations, who
have been institutionalised to focus on organisations’

00:16:34.839 --> 00:16:41.370
behaviour and outcomes, to suddenly say that
what we now need to do is focus on place,

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:49.600
I think is intellectually recognised by most
of my colleagues, but clearly more needs to

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:55.220
be done in order to secure practice and to
secure effective implementation.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:17:03.839
I do, however, genuinely believe that if you
address some of the changes which have already

00:17:03.839 --> 00:17:10.030
been made – around the health innovations
system, which is one very good example.

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:15.199
We achieved in three months what I could never
have achieved in four years by actually establishing

00:17:15.199 --> 00:17:21.220
a place-based strategy, with organisations
fitting into those place-based strategies.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:27.299
If you actually look at the progress which
guys like David and Mike and others have led

00:17:27.299 --> 00:17:34.490
around single-hospital services in Manchester
and what we are beginning to see in other

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:40.380
parts of Greater Manchester, and what we are
seeing from healthcare providers sitting alongside

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:46.820
commissioners with the third sector – as
you will see in every locality in Greater

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:54.799
Manchester – it is starting to change people’s
perceptions around the importance of place

00:17:54.799 --> 00:18:00.660
and how organisational form and delivery need
to fit the priorities of place, rather than

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:02.059
the other way round.

00:18:02.059 --> 00:18:08.870
I am not saying that there will not be some
tensions and frictions along the way; of course

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:09.870
there will.

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:10.870
That is inevitable.

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:16.090
However, I am very convinced that we have
an institutional governance framework which

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:21.400
is going to drive those behaviours, as well.

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:24.330
The other thing, which I perhaps should have
mentioned but this now gives me the opportunity

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:30.890
to mention, is that it has to be people-driven
and worker-driven as well.

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:38.270
We have, I think, exemplified good practice
in Greater Manchester over some time in what

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:43.030
we mean by reform and what we mean by the
behaviours of all our staff who work in local

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:44.140
government.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:50.440
We now have the opportunity to enjoin our
wider health colleagues in that development

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:52.770
and that organisational process.

00:18:52.770 --> 00:19:00.190
Certainly, on the evidence we have seen, many
of the people we are engaging want to be part

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:01.440
of that process.

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:07.840
I think that, perhaps, is one of the more
exciting elements of this endeavour.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:17.590
Noel Gordon&gt;&gt; There is much to admire, Howard,
in the architecture of the governance and

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:24.570
collaboration that you put in place to help
you design and execute this.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:31.000
What you achieve will certainly be examined
carefully by many others in take-off slots

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:34.080
over the next year or two.

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:40.160
I think one of the things they will be most
interested in is what this has allowed you

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:50.370
to achieve in the area of accessing hard-to-reach
minorities, and in particular how to deal

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:56.660
with uniformity of care and better uniformity
of standards.

00:19:56.660 --> 00:20:01.669
What does devo allow you to achieve in that
particular space that you could not have achieved

00:20:01.669 --> 00:20:03.170
under a devo architecture?

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:08.520
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; I think there are two answers
to that.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:14.250
First of all, it gives us a remarkable opportunity
to join up our own programmes.

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:20.030
We have programmes which are geared to complex
dependency in Greater Manchester.

00:20:20.030 --> 00:20:28.380
One of the pilots that was announced by the
devolution agreement in November 2014 was

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:36.669
a 50,000 pilot programme about how we tackle
and support people with complex dependencies

00:20:36.669 --> 00:20:39.610
in Greater Manchester, which is a huge issue.

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:46.760
There have been other pilots announced about
how we support and access health services

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:49.210
to get people into work.

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:55.970
What we now have the opportunity to do, frankly,
through the wider access to health and social

00:20:55.970 --> 00:21:03.250
care service, is join it all up in a very
effective way.

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:09.600
That is why I have emphasised, very importantly,
the critical nature of the single commissioning

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:12.110
hubs in each of the ten localities.

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:20.050
You cannot do the very difficult public service
interaction stuff at a national level, in

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:21.150
my view.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.820
That is why devolution, as a debate, is absolutely
essential.

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:29.480
We believe what we are seeking to do here
by joining up – and there is some evidence,

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:34.520
particularly around early years too, and access
to youngsters’ mental health services – which

00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:38.430
I think will drive very, very positive outcomes.

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:44.740
Michelle Mitchell&gt;&gt; Thank you for your presentation.

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:55.190
I just have a brief question, which is: how
you have engaged citizens, patients and carers

00:21:55.190 --> 00:22:01.490
in co-producing the plans, and how you intend
to do so in the future?

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:03.260
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:09.130
I have some very clear views about this, if
I am being frank, which I will share.

00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:15.799
I am not always convinced that the standard
public consultation arrangements, which we

00:22:15.799 --> 00:22:20.480
are statutorily obliged to undertake at times
when we are talking about GGA[?], actually

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:24.800
genuinely engage real people.

00:22:24.800 --> 00:22:29.929
When we did the Healthier Together programme
last year, I was being told that this was

00:22:29.929 --> 00:22:35.370
one of the most successful public consultation
exercises that people had seen.

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:42.310
I think it covered or engaged less than 1%
of the Greater Manchester population, which

00:22:42.310 --> 00:22:43.890
was still regarded then as significant.

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:50.340
I might have got the numbers wrong, but it
was very insignificant in the context of the

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:51.340
total population.

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:55.130
I believe that engagement is a day-by-day
thing.

00:22:55.130 --> 00:23:01.080
How local authorities and workers engage their
communities is part and parcel of how we work

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:02.400
day by day.

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:07.960
So the way in which our locality plans are
developed – the priorities at local level

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:13.909
– I think, in broad terms, the mix of priorities
might be different, but the broad priorities

00:23:13.909 --> 00:23:16.660
will remain more or less the same.

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:23.190
The way in which we bring forward detailed
changes to service patterns over the coming

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:31.480
months – we inevitably have to engage people
both directly affected and indirectly affected,

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:37.040
and we need to explore and examine different
ways of engaging our communities.

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:43.870
The overall strategy we are trying to bring
forward puts that right at the heart of our

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:45.059
overall strategy.

00:23:45.059 --> 00:23:51.030
In addition to that, the traditional consultation
approaches to the strategic plan – there

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:54.690
is information within this paper which shows
that it has been noticed.

00:23:54.690 --> 00:24:01.070
We have got responses, but for me it is the
day-to-day engagement with our communities

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:07.380
which I think is going to be an absolutely
essential feature of our programme in the

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:08.380
future.

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:09.950
John Burn&gt;&gt; Wonderful presentation.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:16.185
It is not a formal declaration of interest,
but I am a clinical academic in Newcastle,

00:24:16.185 --> 00:24:18.960
so I can declare that I am interested.

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:26.070
I am interested both from the context of the
‘Northern Powerhouse’ and northern integration,

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:32.090
but also as a clinician for the Northeast
and Cumbria: the place impact, in a sense,

00:24:32.090 --> 00:24:35.070
on surrounding places.

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:40.600
So the question, from an NHS perspective,
that I am most interested in is: what do you

00:24:40.600 --> 00:24:46.150
envisage the impact of this being, intended
or otherwise, on neighbouring areas?

00:24:46.150 --> 00:24:51.890
Clearly you have a very discrete and, as you
said, very integrated unit in Greater Manchester,

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:56.760
but I shared care for a long time with patients
in south Cumbria.

00:24:56.760 --> 00:25:03.060
So what do you envisage the impact, positive
or negative, of this integration being on

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:06.419
those other, more disparate populations?

00:25:06.419 --> 00:25:13.360
Do you see them becoming another devolutionary
group, or do you see them gradually migrating

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:19.659
into the black hole of gravity, if you see
what I mean; that Manchester gets ever bigger?

00:25:19.659 --> 00:25:24.440
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; Well, that’s an interesting
thought.

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:28.520
I think there are two points, really.

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:37.100
Firstly, if some of my NHS colleagues were
here, they would say very clearly that the

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:41.990
way in which we plan and deliver our service
transformation programmes in Greater Manchester

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:47.460
must take clear cognisance of the fact that
there are people outside Greater Manchester

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:53.669
which actually access some of our big specialist
hospitals and services.

00:25:53.669 --> 00:25:59.029
We will work very hard to protect those.

00:25:59.029 --> 00:26:02.950
We are on a journey.

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:05.950
I am not saying we have all the answers to
that journey.

00:26:05.950 --> 00:26:11.510
This is difficult stuff, if I am being really
frank with you.

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:18.470
For me, I think this journey is a joint one
with colleagues from the National Health Service.

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:25.980
I think we have demonstrated that by sharing
a vision and by allowing the leadership – and

00:26:25.980 --> 00:26:32.700
there is leadership within the NHS, in different
places, as well as in local government – and

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:40.520
encouraging that leadership to emerge, we
can show that is possible to expeditiously

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:50.900
drive forward a programme and secure stakeholder
commitment to the delivery of that programme.

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:59.300
In those terms, what works as a functional
economic geography might make sense at a functional

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:03.830
health economy level, if that is the right
phrase.

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:10.529
There are opportunities for other parts of
the country to learn what we do well and learn

00:27:10.529 --> 00:27:15.600
from the mistakes we make – because we will
make some mistakes, as much as we all abhor

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:16.600
that.

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:19.020
I think it is inevitable when you are doing
things at this scale.

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:24.070
Not everything is going to work to the level
that we would hope.

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:30.890
I think, in those terms, there are huge opportunities
for health/social care, particularly with

00:27:30.890 --> 00:27:36.960
the advent of the Better Care Fund coming
through in 2018/19, to be able to drive this

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:38.640
integration process.

00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:45.800
What it means for the Northern Powerhouse
is that I think, in economic terms, we will

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:47.789
have a more productive labour market.

00:27:47.789 --> 00:27:52.600
A more productive labour market means we are
more economically competitive.

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:59.490
That means the North of England will be able,
I believe, to punch its full weight economically,

00:27:59.490 --> 00:28:03.210
not just within the UK but in the global market.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:11.529
Moira Gibb&gt;&gt; I would like to commend you and
your colleagues, Howard, for your strenuous

00:28:11.529 --> 00:28:14.240
efforts on this front.

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:18.360
We have had lots of attempts over the year,
have we not, in health and local government

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:20.020
to work better together.

00:28:20.020 --> 00:28:25.430
A question, really, is: what do you need us
and the other national bodies to do differently,

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:27.010
or to stop doing, perhaps?

00:28:27.010 --> 00:28:30.820
That would be interesting to hear.

00:28:30.820 --> 00:28:39.730
Also, in paragraph 31 you refer to experiencing
the first effects in relation to the Better

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:41.299
Care Fund, which you have just mentioned.

00:28:41.299 --> 00:28:47.409
It would be really good to hear, because that
has not been the easiest of collaborative

00:28:47.409 --> 00:28:55.820
efforts around the country, if you can tell
us a little bit about how it is making a difference

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:56.820
already.

00:28:56.820 --> 00:28:58.610
Howard Bernstein: Yes.

00:28:58.610 --> 00:29:03.760
It is interesting; the Better Care Fund, when
it comes forward in – 2018/19?

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:09.080
Carol Culley&gt;&gt; A little bit in 2017/18, but
the new money predominantly comes through

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:10.080
in 2018/19.

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:13.919
Howard Bernstein: Yes, well that is two years
too late for us, really, because we are trying

00:29:13.919 --> 00:29:15.820
to do it now.

00:29:15.820 --> 00:29:25.621
So the flexibility that we got given to elected
politicians to increase council tax to support

00:29:25.621 --> 00:29:31.630
social care spend, all ten local authorities
in Greater Manchester agreed to do that.

00:29:31.630 --> 00:29:37.760
All ten local authorities in Greater Manchester
agreed to create a common platform for commissioning

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:38.760
health and social care.

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:43.270
We want to do things once, wherever we can,
rather than ten times.

00:29:43.270 --> 00:29:46.179
That has never been done before.

00:29:46.179 --> 00:29:52.210
The commitment to pooling social care spend
alongside NHS commissioning spend has never

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:55.020
been done to the scale that it has been now.

00:29:55.020 --> 00:30:01.929
That is starting to drive massively different
new approaches to the way in which we commission

00:30:01.929 --> 00:30:04.880
local services.

00:30:04.880 --> 00:30:11.559
By the end of the first year, I know the leadership
of Greater Manchester will be very disappointed

00:30:11.559 --> 00:30:19.710
if we are not just commissioning health/social
care in significant scale, but also other

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:22.700
services: early years and children, as well
as public health.

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:28.649
I think that is the huge prize which devolution
of health/social care has given to Greater

00:30:28.649 --> 00:30:32.460
Manchester and its commitment to working generally.

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:40.409
Carol Culley&gt;&gt; I think one of the other issues
alluded to is how we are working with some

00:30:40.409 --> 00:30:44.690
of the national assurance processes and things
in place to see them through a GM lens.

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:49.720
For example, GM is now a unit of planning
with the existing Better Care Fund programme.

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:54.150
It is how we can use that as a lever to drive
improvement and support, what we are doing

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:59.940
in locality plans and the GM strategic plan,
as well as looking at it by an organisational

00:30:59.940 --> 00:31:04.059
basis, which is how it has tended to work
in the past.

00:31:04.059 --> 00:31:11.220
Howard Bernstein: Jim Mackey is appointing
a relationship manager to sit in the devolution

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:16.460
team in Greater Manchester to facilitate that
day-to-day contact.

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:20.100
We are enjoying very strong working relationships
with CQC.

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:25.649
I think the challenge for all of us, actually,
is that as we come up with all our new initiatives

00:31:25.649 --> 00:31:31.039
month by month and year by year, we need to
ensure that places like Greater Manchester

00:31:31.039 --> 00:31:37.690
are properly recognised in the way in which
those initiatives are framed.

00:31:37.690 --> 00:31:46.340
Otherwise we could find ourselves, not necessarily
operating in conflict, but not securing the

00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:49.610
maximum synergy from our collective efforts.

00:31:49.610 --> 00:31:56.080
Wendy Becker&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, that was
very interesting.

00:31:56.080 --> 00:32:02.990
There were clearly a number of things you
have been doing, and tremendous progress that

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:03.990
has been made.

00:32:03.990 --> 00:32:08.090
The question I had is about the balance across
local integration and things done at scale

00:32:08.090 --> 00:32:13.860
across Greater Manchester, and really taking
control of things done outside of Greater

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:15.110
Manchester.

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:18.980
What do you see as the really large, pan-GM
plays?

00:32:18.980 --> 00:32:22.299
What are the largest themes that you are facing?

00:32:22.299 --> 00:32:30.559
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; I think population health
is a very clear theme, and we are not going

00:32:30.559 --> 00:32:33.120
to impact on that overnight.

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:42.110
It is not just around the quality of our programmes,
which I think will determine our long-term

00:32:42.110 --> 00:32:43.110
success.

00:32:43.110 --> 00:32:48.990
I think we can all assume that the way in
which we develop early-years programmes, as

00:32:48.990 --> 00:32:57.789
one particular example, will be hugely impactful,
but also how we create what I would describe

00:32:57.789 --> 00:33:00.809
as the social movement for change.

00:33:00.809 --> 00:33:08.450
Picking up on some of the points I talked
about earlier, this is also an opportunity

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:13.750
for local authorities to rethink their relationships
with the people who live and work in their

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:14.750
places.

00:33:14.750 --> 00:33:22.019
It is not just about what local authorities
or other public service providers can do for

00:33:22.019 --> 00:33:27.220
the people who live in those places; it is
also, in return, what people are going to

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:32.820
do for themselves and to improve their own
life chances as well.

00:33:32.820 --> 00:33:37.850
We are seeing different manifestations of
that in different parts of Greater Manchester,

00:33:37.850 --> 00:33:41.070
but all rooted in the same deal.

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:47.350
We have seen stuff like that in Manchester,
it is going on in Wigan and in other parts

00:33:47.350 --> 00:33:48.510
of the area.

00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:57.669
That could be and should be the real game
changer in the way in which we actually renew

00:33:57.669 --> 00:34:02.270
our relationships between the citizen and
the state.

00:34:02.270 --> 00:34:06.049
Margaret Casely-Hayford&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:34:06.049 --> 00:34:10.310
I am full of admiration for the ambition here.

00:34:10.310 --> 00:34:18.990
I can see that, across the country, there
will be a great deal of attention paid to

00:34:18.990 --> 00:34:24.470
its success or not, with a view to perhaps
replicating the model.

00:34:24.470 --> 00:34:33.070
What I was wondering is, given that you talk
about subsidiarity and different elements,

00:34:33.070 --> 00:34:44.669
and we also want to see accountability, there
is going to be an interesting architecture

00:34:44.669 --> 00:34:55.450
to allow people to be able to get the right
level of accountability and really understand

00:34:55.450 --> 00:35:06.390
how they can see the success of the services,
and hold to account those who need to be held

00:35:06.390 --> 00:35:07.480
to account.

00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:15.190
The clarity of that architecture, I think,
is what is going to enable the success of

00:35:15.190 --> 00:35:17.480
this to be replicated.

00:35:17.480 --> 00:35:25.390
You very clearly and, I thought, quite rightly,
responded to a question about co-creation

00:35:25.390 --> 00:35:30.670
and the part the citizen plays in it by saying
that you would involve citizens on a day-to-day

00:35:30.670 --> 00:35:31.670
basis.

00:35:31.670 --> 00:35:36.650
I can understand that when it comes to co-creation
upfront, but when it comes to accountability,

00:35:36.650 --> 00:35:44.300
I suppose I am asking if there is a need for
something more formulaic at a higher level

00:35:44.300 --> 00:35:50.560
in terms of really understanding how the citizen
gets the response that they require, that

00:35:50.560 --> 00:35:51.560
we require?

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:54.920
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; I would have thought that
was local government, actually.

00:35:54.920 --> 00:36:03.520
I know local government is a system which
has not always enjoyed widespread approval,

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:10.800
but in the context of health/social care you
have three democratically elected councillors

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:18.150
in each ward, and you have democratically
elected local authorities taking responsibility

00:36:18.150 --> 00:36:25.290
for working with providers and NHS colleagues
in producing locality plans.

00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:30.670
You have a strategic partnership board chaired
by a member of the combined authorities, with

00:36:30.670 --> 00:36:35.420
all the members of the Greater Manchester
combined authority leaders on that.

00:36:35.420 --> 00:36:40.230
I think that is pretty transparent and accountable.

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:48.130
If you compare that with the current system,
which I do not think feels that close to people

00:36:48.130 --> 00:36:57.800
at all, certainly in transparency and accountability
terms and certainly at the front line, I think

00:36:57.800 --> 00:37:04.680
what we are creating is something which can
transform those relationships and what people

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:10.290
feel are properly accountable relationships
with local councillors and others.

00:37:10.290 --> 00:37:17.050
My final point is that I am not saying the
system we have now is absolutely perfect.

00:37:17.050 --> 00:37:21.730
I think it is far more accountable than what
we have had before.

00:37:21.730 --> 00:37:26.770
I am not saying there may not be a case, as
we go down this journey, where there could

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:35.500
be further changes, or where changes to that
process would not become justified.

00:37:35.500 --> 00:37:40.650
Where we are at the moment, I think, is a
pretty good place and a very strong platform

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:41.760
for moving forward.

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:47.070
David Roberts&gt;&gt; As somebody who has lived
and worked in Manchester previously in my

00:37:47.070 --> 00:37:52.930
career, I stand in awe at the transformation
of the city over the last 20 years.

00:37:52.930 --> 00:37:59.150
I think this is another element in that continuing
transformation into a vibrant and economically

00:37:59.150 --> 00:38:01.390
sustainable entity.

00:38:01.390 --> 00:38:09.620
Therefore, my question is a simple one: we
have 43 other transformation areas, or STP

00:38:09.620 --> 00:38:13.430
areas, which are going to be following, in
some way, shape or form, some of the things

00:38:13.430 --> 00:38:22.079
you have done; what are the one, two or three
pieces of advice you would give to your fellow

00:38:22.079 --> 00:38:25.600
leaders of those entities, and to us?

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:29.150
Howard Bernstein: You certainly have to have
a sense of humour.

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:30.280
I think that is absolutely key.

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:35.070
David Roberts&gt;&gt; Having been a Liverpudlian
working in Manchester, I can tell you I have

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:36.700
one of those.

00:38:36.700 --> 00:38:44.960
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; Apart from that, it is
very hard to think that you can just start

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:48.270
talking about collaboration and say, ‘Right,
we have to collaborate from tomorrow, and

00:38:48.270 --> 00:38:51.250
we are going to do all of this.’

00:38:51.250 --> 00:38:56.220
We found it hard enough, and collaboration
and partnership is part of our DNA.

00:38:56.220 --> 00:38:58.430
David Roberts&gt;&gt; 2020, it is a long time.

00:38:58.430 --> 00:39:00.369
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; It is a long time.

00:39:00.369 --> 00:39:06.530
Therefore, this has to assume there is personal
responsibility of the leaders of places.

00:39:06.530 --> 00:39:12.790
This is not something you can delegate down
and say, at third or fourth tier level, ‘Right,

00:39:12.790 --> 00:39:13.980
go and make that happen.’

00:39:13.980 --> 00:39:15.400
It is not going to happen.

00:39:15.400 --> 00:39:24.089
This has to be a joint responsibility, and
one which is wanting to be assumed by the

00:39:24.089 --> 00:39:25.950
leaders of those places.

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:28.970
That does not just mean elected leaders.

00:39:28.970 --> 00:39:37.810
We are hugely fortunate in GM, because we
have some of the most energetic and well-known

00:39:37.810 --> 00:39:42.580
leaders in local government, frankly, and
we have had them for many, many years.

00:39:42.580 --> 00:39:46.750
It is the executive leadership in place; not
just me, but throughout Greater Manchester.

00:39:46.750 --> 00:39:51.680
All my chief executive colleagues know what
a locality plan looks like.

00:39:51.680 --> 00:39:54.890
They know what should be in a locality plan.

00:39:54.890 --> 00:40:00.030
They are charged, by me and others, to go
away and make sure that within their own localities

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:06.530
they are working with CCG chief officers and
chairs, and with providers, to ensure those

00:40:06.530 --> 00:40:08.910
locality plans are brought forward.

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:14.200
I spend a lot of my time, with colleagues
from the devolution team, working with the

00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:17.780
leaders of the key stakeholders.

00:40:17.780 --> 00:40:22.359
Without that leadership and commitment, it
is not going to happen, frankly.

00:40:22.359 --> 00:40:24.849
It has to be part of the day job.

00:40:24.849 --> 00:40:25.849
David Roberts&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:40:25.849 --> 00:40:27.579
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Howard, thank you so much
for those responses.

00:40:27.579 --> 00:40:32.680
I am now going to invite Paul to talk about
the specific delegation arrangements.

00:40:32.680 --> 00:40:37.720
Paul Baumann&gt;&gt; I will keep this brief as well,
because you have the papers in front of you.

00:40:37.720 --> 00:40:41.680
In the light of those ambitions and the very
clear next steps that I think Howard has talked

00:40:41.680 --> 00:40:47.250
about delivering, we do now need today to
conclude the formal business of enacting the

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:48.260
delegation arrangements.

00:40:48.260 --> 00:40:53.410
Otherwise, none of what you have heard in
the last half an hour will be deliverable,

00:40:53.410 --> 00:40:57.270
because we have to make those changes.

00:40:57.270 --> 00:41:01.420
The things that we are going to decide upon
today underpin the form of devolution, and

00:41:01.420 --> 00:41:06.060
it is quite a specific form of devolution
at this stage, which frankly enables us to

00:41:06.060 --> 00:41:10.280
get on with all the exciting things that you
have just heard Howard talk about without

00:41:10.280 --> 00:41:15.190
getting entrammelled in vastly complex changes
of accountability, governance and all the

00:41:15.190 --> 00:41:16.190
rest of it.

00:41:16.190 --> 00:41:19.070
We have adopted a particular form for it.

00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:24.980
Devo, for now, will take the form of the Greater
Manchester Chief Officer, with a newly formed

00:41:24.980 --> 00:41:31.730
team including relevant staff resource from
the NHS England teams in the locality, undertaking

00:41:31.730 --> 00:41:37.140
the business which NHS England currently does,
but within Greater Manchester and as an integral

00:41:37.140 --> 00:41:42.710
part of the Greater Manchester team – indeed,
at the lead of the Greater Manchester team

00:41:42.710 --> 00:41:47.920
– with formal accountability, of course,
to NHS England, as is necessary given the

00:41:47.920 --> 00:41:48.920
functions.

00:41:48.920 --> 00:41:51.450
That will be exercised through me on behalf
of the Board here.

00:41:51.450 --> 00:41:56.990
Equally, if not more, critical is an accountability
to the local strategic partnership board,

00:41:56.990 --> 00:42:02.720
to Howard and to the other leaders who are
making things happen locally.

00:42:02.720 --> 00:42:06.910
Subject to necessary safeguards, which are
all in the documents which you are referred

00:42:06.910 --> 00:42:12.800
to, we want to give as much real power of
decision-making to people in Greater Manchester

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:17.240
as possible, notwithstanding the niceties
of it being NHS England business which is

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:20.829
being, in many cases, transacted.

00:42:20.829 --> 00:42:24.369
That will take place in the context of a joint
commissioning board which is being set up

00:42:24.369 --> 00:42:30.630
precisely for that purpose, to enable everything,
wherever possible, to be decided at local

00:42:30.630 --> 00:42:35.240
level in response to the strategies that you
have heard described there, and with full

00:42:35.240 --> 00:42:40.280
public engagement, which again is one of the
key features of what you talked about, Howard.

00:42:40.280 --> 00:42:44.400
The formal paper you have in front of you
– the less exciting of the four or five

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:48.920
papers, I have to say – sets out the decisions
that we need to take as a Board to enable

00:42:48.920 --> 00:42:52.921
that to happen progressively from tomorrow,
to remind people of the timeline that we are

00:42:52.921 --> 00:42:54.600
working to here.

00:42:54.600 --> 00:42:58.690
There are two things for us to do today, set
out in paragraph 2, following which Simon

00:42:58.690 --> 00:43:04.330
will then nominate the GM chief officer as
the relevant person to exercise the delegated

00:43:04.330 --> 00:43:06.700
functions with regard to Greater Manchester.

00:43:06.700 --> 00:43:13.310
It is worth just saying, in parentheses, that
we have undertaken, as you would expect, a

00:43:13.310 --> 00:43:17.900
thorough review along the lines of what we
have talked to both the Commissioning Committee

00:43:17.900 --> 00:43:22.369
and the Board about, as to the readiness of
Greater Manchester to take on the delegation

00:43:22.369 --> 00:43:27.170
and devolution that we have got, using the
very explicit assessment framework which the

00:43:27.170 --> 00:43:30.920
Commissioning Committee in particular has
helped us to develop.

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:37.510
The conclusions reached by the executive were
universally positive that the Greater Manchester

00:43:37.510 --> 00:43:41.240
devolution was ready to happen in the terms
that we are setting it out, and that view

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:45.190
was supported by the Commissioning Committee,
which reviewed this over the course of the

00:43:45.190 --> 00:43:46.900
last few weeks.

00:43:46.900 --> 00:43:50.240
In moving forward, it has had all the relevant
assessments.

00:43:50.240 --> 00:43:56.490
Turning to our task for today, you can see
at paragraph 13 the list of functions that

00:43:56.490 --> 00:44:02.870
we are proposing to delegate to Greater Manchester,
in the form of the chief officer and his team.

00:44:02.870 --> 00:44:09.030
As you would expect, they are mostly concerned
with commissioning and assurance roles, which

00:44:09.030 --> 00:44:11.780
are the main things that we do on a local
basis.

00:44:11.780 --> 00:44:16.400
There are, however, some other quite important
statutory functions, including revalidation,

00:44:16.400 --> 00:44:20.420
performer regulation, complaints and emergency
response.

00:44:20.420 --> 00:44:24.450
As you would expect, we have paid particular
attention to these in our assessment of readiness

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:27.060
and can again confirm that it is all in order.

00:44:27.060 --> 00:44:31.780
You will see that a few of the functions,
shown at paragraph 17, will have a delayed

00:44:31.780 --> 00:44:39.200
transfer to GM control, either to enable safe
handover, as with the emergency preparedness,

00:44:39.200 --> 00:44:43.310
or because there are close-down functions
that need to be performed for 2015/16 before

00:44:43.310 --> 00:44:48.510
we hand over the reins of, for example, the
accounting processes to their new owners,

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:51.020
if I can put it that way.

00:44:51.020 --> 00:44:56.390
The formal mode by which we effect all of
those changes is set out in paragraphs 18

00:44:56.390 --> 00:44:58.270
to 21 of the paper.

00:44:58.270 --> 00:45:02.570
The big benefit of the route we are taking
is that, when other people catch up with Greater

00:45:02.570 --> 00:45:07.440
Manchester, we have a form of delegation that
we have done here which we can readily replicate,

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:11.819
once we have done the assessment that I have
talked about for the next places, to the extent

00:45:11.819 --> 00:45:14.420
that they follow this particular path of devolution.

00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:20.010
We do not need to keep coming back to the
Board and asking for a different form of delegation

00:45:20.010 --> 00:45:22.190
routine.

00:45:22.190 --> 00:45:25.570
For those of you who are really excited by
minutiae – and I did notice my Chief Executive

00:45:25.570 --> 00:45:29.730
was reading it during the course of the more
interesting parts of the debate – appendix

00:45:29.730 --> 00:45:35.329
B sets out the actual delegation instrument,
including the specific list of statutory functions

00:45:35.329 --> 00:45:36.630
and the timings of transfer.

00:45:36.630 --> 00:45:41.470
You can rest assured; as I say, the Chief
Executive has personally scrutinised the commas

00:45:41.470 --> 00:45:44.760
and full stops, and will sign that, if you
let him.

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:50.630
At the same time, we will amend the SFIs of
NHS England to give the Chief Officer similar

00:45:50.630 --> 00:45:55.430
financial limits to a national or regional
director, to enable him to do the job that

00:45:55.430 --> 00:45:57.200
is needed.

00:45:57.200 --> 00:46:01.770
Self-evidently, we will be happy to take any
questions you might have on this exciting

00:46:01.770 --> 00:46:06.550
material, but in the absence of such questions
I would ask you to support the resolutions

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:12.550
in the paper to enable Howard and his many
friends locally, with our active support,

00:46:12.550 --> 00:46:16.440
to get on with the exciting task that he has
set out.

00:46:16.440 --> 00:46:21.329
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; On the assumption that there
are no questions on the exciting material,

00:46:21.329 --> 00:46:29.380
may I invite the Board to approve, first of
all, the proposed summary list of NHS England

00:46:29.380 --> 00:46:32.839
functions to be internally delegated to the
GM Chief Officer?

00:46:32.839 --> 00:46:34.190
Thank you.

00:46:34.190 --> 00:46:39.579
Secondly, to approve the proposed changes
to our reservation of powers and delegation

00:46:39.579 --> 00:46:45.440
of functions and standing financial instructions?

00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:46.640
Thank you.

00:46:46.640 --> 00:46:50.290
I think that brings us to the end of this
item of business.

00:46:50.290 --> 00:46:58.069
Simply to say, on behalf of the whole Board,
to Howard and to Carol, many thanks for accepting

00:46:58.069 --> 00:46:59.720
our invitation to join us this morning.

00:46:59.720 --> 00:47:05.760
It has allowed us to understand much more
intimately what lies ahead of you, and the

00:47:05.760 --> 00:47:07.300
challenges that you are taking on.

00:47:07.300 --> 00:47:11.470
Secondly, just to record that this is truly
an historic moment.

00:47:11.470 --> 00:47:17.460
This is the first time that a body such as
this has delegated such significant powers

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:23.200
to an organisation which already has spent
years on the building blocks to bring together

00:47:23.200 --> 00:47:26.150
the local authorities and to be able to pool
budgets and responsibilities.

00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:34.390
Thirdly, it is historic in the sense that,
as Paul has just outlined, we have a precedent.

00:47:34.390 --> 00:47:40.030
The eyes of the world are on you, and we wish
you well and look forward to working with

00:47:40.030 --> 00:47:44.570
you very closely in partnership to bring off
the vision that you have outlined to us this

00:47:44.570 --> 00:47:45.790
morning.

00:47:45.790 --> 00:47:47.150
Thank you again for attending our meeting;
we are very grateful.

00:47:47.150 --> 00:47:47.760
Howard Bernstein&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for
the invite.

